Exactly. Most of what we think we “know” about another person is inference layered over a very small observation surface.

You can observe behavior, words, timing, posture, choices, maybe some history—but you usually cannot directly know motive, private context, internal tradeoffs, what information they had, what they noticed, what they misunderstood, or what state they were in when they acted.

That makes a useful calibration rule:

\text{observation} \neq \text{interpretation} \neq \text{person}

You can still infer. You just keep the inference probabilistic and update it when reality pushes back. The same tune-as-you-play rule applies to people: form a model, test it gently, watch the delta, and resist turning an early hypothesis into identity.

A lot of avoidable error comes from collapsing “I saw X” into “therefore they are Y.”